<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  RealSense mounting link for the PHYSICAL RX-150.

  This is the hardware counterpart to rx150_gripper_depth_camera.urdf.xacro.
  That file is SIM ONLY: it carries a Gazebo <sensor> plugin that fabricates a
  depth camera. On the real arm the RealSense driver produces the data, and all
  the robot description has to do is say WHERE the camera is bolted on.

  Why this file has to exist at all: without a camera link in the URDF,
  robot_state_publisher publishes no camera frame, so the RealSense's own TF
  tree (camera_link -> camera_depth_optical_frame -> ...) is a free-floating
  island with no path to rx150/base_link. Every TF lookup from the camera frame
  then fails, which silently kills BOTH consumers: the sweep relay
  (scene_point_cloud, so no obstacle map) and localization_3d_node (so vision
  publishes nothing and the mission aborts at the vision phase).

  ##########################################################################
  THE NUMBERS BELOW ARE PLACEHOLDERS. MEASURE THEM ON THE REAL ARM.
  ##########################################################################
  They are copied from the sim camera's invented offset and are almost
  certainly wrong for your bracket. See TASK_camera_extrinsics_urdf.md. Until
  they are measured, the point cloud will land in the wrong place relative to
  the arm: consistently and confidently, which is the hard kind of wrong.

  How to check: put a known object in front of the arm, look at
  /planning/live_point_cloud in RViz with the robot model shown, and confirm
  the cloud lands where the object physically is. If it is offset or tilted,
  it is these numbers, not the pipeline.

  xyz is metres from the parent link's origin; rpy is roll/pitch/yaw in radians.

  ==========================================================================
  KEEP COMMENTS INSIDE <robot> SHORT AND FREE OF A COLON FOLLOWED BY A SPACE.
  ==========================================================================
  xacro strips comments that sit outside <robot> (like this one) but PRESERVES
  the ones inside it. The generated URDF is handed to robot_state_publisher as
  a string parameter, and launch_ros YAML-parses it on the way through. One
  "colon space" inside a surviving comment turns the whole description into a
  YAML mapping, and the entire hardware launch dies with

      Unable to parse the value of parameter robot_description as yaml

  which names neither this file nor the comment. bcr_arm_rx150/test/
  test_camera_tf_chain.py asserts the rendered URDF still loads as a plain
  string, so a regression here fails a test instead of a bring-up.

  The seam with the RealSense driver's TF tree:
  perception.launch.py publishes a static transform from camera_mount_link to
  the driver's root frame (camera_link by default), so the driver's internal
  optical frames inherit a path back to rx150/base_link. That static transform
  is deliberately IDENTITY; the real mounting pose belongs in the joint below,
  so the extrinsics have exactly one home. A D435's own frames sit a few cm
  inside its casing, so if you measure to the casing, fold that offset in here.
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!-- Mounting pose, shared with the sim description so the two cannot drift
       apart. PLACEHOLDER, measure it. -->
  <xacro:include filename="$(find bcr_arm_rx150)/urdf/camera_mount_pose.xacro"/>

  <joint name="$(arg robot_name)_camera_mount" type="fixed">
    <parent link="$(arg robot_name)/gripper_link"/>
    <child  link="$(arg robot_name)/camera_mount_link"/>
    <origin xyz="${cam_mount_xyz}" rpy="${cam_mount_rpy}"/>
  </joint>

  <link name="$(arg robot_name)/camera_mount_link">
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.025 0.090 0.025"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="interbotix_black"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.025 0.090 0.025"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <mass value="0.072"/>
      <inertia
        ixx="0.00001" iyy="0.00001" izz="0.00001"
        ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyz="0.0"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>

</robot>
